e in Paris on a hundred dollars a  month I mean today you barely pays for  lunch and so I kind of hung out if you  want I mean I enrolled in school in  order to get my GI Bill did go to  classes did get the required diplomas  and so forth of which which are really  valuable in a way but by some chance I  got hired by Time magazine first as a  spear carrier and a general factotum I  spoke French by this stage and  eventually became a correspondent of  time and that was the beginning of my  professional career back in 1950 I want  to get to some more of that and some of  the work you do for the Washington Post  and others but first one I asked you  first show the audience what your book  looks like and by now they've probably  seen in the bookstores they've seen the  part of the television show that you're  doing with PBS the first question I want  to ask you and I know this book is about  the Philippines right down the bottom  author of Vietnam history that book also  was made into a television series on PBS  where did you get the idea to combine  your books with television well I felt  that that television could do a lot of  things in telling a story I mean you  can't you can't really dramatize certain  events in words  in the same way you can you could do it  on the screen I mean the old Chinese  adage about a picture being worth a  thousand words I think is is modest I  think a good picture might be worth a  whole chapter in a book and certain  events very dramatic events are just an  almost impossible to capture in words  but television has its limitations it's  very hard in a ongoing documentary to  analyze things television requires  images and visual visuals it requires  photographs or film for example if  you're trying to deal with a diplomatic  negotiation what have you got on  television what have you got to deal  with usually you can't get into the room  to listen to people bargaining so you  either have the opening shot that the  negotiators allow the tel