ome truly independent  from the United States because their  independence the independence they got  in 1946 was a kind of dependent  independence they depended on us for aid  they depend on us for advice they depend  us on numerous occasions to help them  put pull the chestnuts out of the fire  they're depending on us right now for  help  fighting the insurgency so they have to  make it on their own and that's going to  be the major challenge for them and I  think it will color their relationship  with us they have to be able to stand up  as a truly sovereign nation this is what  the cover of the book looks like Stanley  Carnot is our guest title is in our  image America's Empire and the  Philippines how much are they living in  our image the image is there when we  went out there we try to turn the  Filipinos into facsimile Americans and  we did to a certain extent  Filipinos copied our music they copied  our dress we taught English and English  is still the common language of the  country they young Filipinos dream of  going to American universities that  gives them a big cachet when they come  home but deep down they still have their  own values and their values are  different from ours we have a society  that we like to think is based on civic  virtue on impersonal institutions  Philippine society is based on personal  relationships based on family ties and  ritual kinship ties and they march to a  different drum be from us I'm not going  to judge whether this is better or not  so when you look back on this hundred  years almost a hundred years of this  American Philippine relationship the  Americanization of the Philippines  Americanized the Filipinos but didn't  make them Americans you born in New York  City right 1925 right studied got a  degree from Harvard that's right they  did also the Sorbonne in Paris but a lot  of your writings in the last several  years had been out in the Far East where  did you get your interest well I started  out as a reporter in